    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if i'm doing this to lower my cortisol, is it basically just about training my body to switch from fight or flight back to a calmer state? do you know if there's a specific way the vagus nerve communicates that change to the brain?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, box breathing can help your body shift from a state of hyperarousal to relaxation by triggering the PNS. The vagus nerve plays a key role in this process, as it helps regulate the body's autonomic nervous system, including the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems.\n\nWhen you breathe deeply and slowly, the vagus nerve can stimulate the release of neurotransmitters such as GABA and serotonin, which help calm the brain and body. The vagus nerve also communicates with the brain's default mode network (DMN), which is responsible for defaulting to a relaxing state, reducing stress and anxiety.",
      "source": "target",
